\newcommand{\fsq}{\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor}
\newcommand{\asc}{\mathrm{asc}}
\newcommand{\desc}{\mathrm{desc}}
\newcommand{\Sham}{\mathrm{Sham}}

\title[Position Pays]{Position Pays: The Balance Law $1/(\sqrt r-1)$ for Sqrt-Descending
Trial Division and the Stratum Beyond the Residue Cap}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study how much the \emph{order} in which trial divisors are visited affects the cost of
trial division on semiprimes $N=pq$ with primes $p\le q$. Costs are counted as expected
numbers of divisibility tests. The candidate pool $\{2,\dots,\fsq\}$ is fixed and only the
visitation order changes. For the ascending order and the \emph{sqrt-descending} order
(Fermat's order applied to divisibility tests), we prove exact costs $p-1$ and $\fsq+1-p$,
which sum to $\fsq$. We prove a sharp crossover wall at $q=4p$ and the gap bound
$2(\desc-1)\le q-p$. Dropping the floor, the per-instance speedup depends on the balance
ratio $r=q/p$ alone and equals $S(r)=1/(\sqrt r-1)$. This function is strictly decreasing,
unbounded as $r\to1^+$, and exceeds a threshold $k$ exactly on the window
$1<r<(1+1/k)^2$. Against the theorem that no residue-class filter improves expected cost by
more than $4/3$, this yields a \emph{barrier map}. On $1<r<49/16$ position strictly beats
every residue filter, at $r=49/16$ the two are equal, and beyond it position is capped by
$4/3$. A \emph{separation theorem} explains why there is no conflict: some order strictly
beats a cyclic-shift sham if and only if the target's marginal is non-uniform. We also show
that Fermat's order is Bayes-optimal for every prior increasing towards $\sqrt N$. These
results account for a sham-controlled measurement of a $5.19\times$ expected speedup (sham
$1.65\times$). We prove that its two mechanisms are orthogonal: truncation at a lower bound
$L$ saves exactly $L-2$ ascending tests and no descending tests. We also prove stratum bands
$[4+2\sqrt5,\infty)$, $[1+\sqrt2,4+2\sqrt5]$ and $[1,1+\sqrt2]$, which contain the measured
values $20.67$, $4.74$ and $1.97$.
\end{abstract}

